[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## C behaviors that had to be reproduced, and were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`run` is called twice on the same `house_t`.** State accumulates instead of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    resetting, so the second call starts from floors=3, bathrooms=3.5, and adds
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `extra_bedrooms` a second time. The transcript is always 8 lines ending at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    floors=4, bedrooms=`5 + 2x`, bathrooms=4.5. Pinned literally in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `state_carries_between_the_two_run_calls`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **`fgets` does not read across newlines.** Only the first line is ever seen;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `"1\n2\n"` yields x=1, not 12 as `scanf` semantics would give. The Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `fgets_stdin` reads byte-at-a-time and stops after the first `\n`, keeping it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **The 99-byte cap is observable in the parsed value.** `char in[100]` with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `fgets(in, sizeof(in), stdin)` stores at most 99 bytes plus a NUL. 91 spaces
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    followed by `123456789` parses as `12345678` — the final digit is cut — and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    both programs print bedrooms=24691361 on the last line. 99 spaces followed by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    a digit loses the digit entirely and takes the error path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **Partial conversion is a success, not an error.** The C code only requires
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `endp != str`, so `42abc` is accepted as 42 and `0x10` is accepted as 0 (base
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    10 stops at `x`). A translation using Rust's `str::parse::<i32>()` would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    reject all of these and print `An error occurred` instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. **Two distinct out-of-range rejections.** `2147483648` keeps `errno == 0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    (it fits a 64-bit `long`) and is rejected by the `INT_MAX` comparison, while
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `9223372036854775808` is rejected by the `errno` check after `strtol`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    saturates and sets `ERANGE`. Both print the same message, but the Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `strtol_base10` has to model saturation and the `ERANGE` flag separately to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    get both right, including the asymmetric negative magnitude limit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    (`|LONG_MIN|` = 2^63, one more than `LONG_MAX`). `-9223372036854775808` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    accepted by `strtol` without `ERANGE` and then rejected as below `INT_MIN`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6. **Signed overflow wraps.** `bedrooms += extra_bedrooms` with x = `INT_MAX`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    is UB in C but wraps two's-complement as compiled here; the C binary prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `-2147483644` then `3`. The Rust uses `wrapping_add`, which matches. Plain
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `+` in Rust would panic in debug builds and abort with a stderr message,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    which would fail the stderr and exit-status assertions.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 7. **Input is bytes, not text.** Lone `0xff`, truncated UTF-8 sequences and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    embedded NULs all reach `strtol`. The Rust reads a `Vec<u8>` and truncates at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the first NUL rather than going through `String`, so no lossy conversion or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    UTF-8 error can change the outcome.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 8. **`%.1f` formatting.** `bathrooms` only ever takes 2.5, 3.5 and 4.5, all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    exactly representable, so Rust's `{:.1}` and C's `%.1f` cannot diverge on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    rounding for any reachable value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 9. **Exit status is always 0 and stderr is always empty**, on the success path
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and the error path alike. Asserted independently in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `exit_status_is_zero_and_stderr_empty_on_both_paths`, because a stdout-only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    comparison would not catch a Rust program that exited non-zero.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Status
